window.docContents[193].resolve({"/#Functional-Programming-in-Lean--Introduction--Release-history--January___-2023":{"contents":"This release adds a chapter on monad transformers that includes a description of the imperative features that are available in do-notation.\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Introduction\u0009Release history","header":"January, 2023","id":"/#Functional-Programming-in-Lean--Introduction--Release-history--January___-2023"},"/#Functional-Programming-in-Lean--Overloading-and-Type-Classes--Summary--Type-Classes-for-Common-Syntax":{"contents":"Most infix operators in Lean are overridden with a type class.\nFor instance, the addition operator corresponds to a type class called .\nMost of these operators have a corresponding heterogeneous version, in which the two arguments need not have the same type.\nThese heterogeneous operators are overloaded using a version of the class whose name starts with H, such as .\n\nIndexing syntax is overloaded using a type class called , which involves proofs.\n has two output parameters, which are the type of elements to be extracted from the collection and a function that can be used to determine what counts as evidence that the index value is in bounds for the collection.\nThis evidence is described by a proposition, and Lean attempts to prove this proposition when array indexing is used.\nWhen Lean is unable to check that list or array access operations are in bounds at compile time, the check can be deferred to run time by appending a ? to the indexing syntax.\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Overloading and Type Classes\u0009Summary","header":"3.8.2. Type Classes for Common Syntax","id":"/#Functional-Programming-in-Lean--Overloading-and-Type-Classes--Summary--Type-Classes-for-Common-Syntax"}});